From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dariusz Majnert <dariusz.majnert@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 14:09:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] task: drop interrupted MDI call state whenever the
 interpreter is reset

An MDI command that runs a remap (or an O-word call) and has to wait for
its queue to drain leaves task with continuation state: mdi_execute_level,
mdi_execute_wait and mdi_execute_next, which make task call
interp.execute(NULL) to resume the call once the queue is empty.
mdi_execute_abort() clears that state, but only some abort paths call it.

emcTaskAbort() closes and resets the interpreter, which unwinds the call
stack, yet the callers in emctask.cc cannot reach the static
mdi_execute_abort(): emcTaskSetState() for E-stop, machine off and E-stop
reset, emcTaskSetMode() for MDI and AUTO, the ON to not-ON edge in
emcTaskUpdate() and the error path of emcTaskPlanSynch(). An
EMC_TASK_PLAN_INIT re-initializes the interpreter without any of it. In
all of these the stale state survives, and when the machine is back on in
MDI mode task resumes a call that no longer exists. For a Python remap
that segfaults milltask in Interp::execute_call() (no executing remap);
for an NGC remap the MDI command never finishes. Commands queued behind
the interrupted one also survived and ran after the machine was
re-enabled.

Make mdi_execute_abort() visible to emctask.cc and call it at the end of
emcTaskAbort(), right after the interpreter reset, and before
emcTaskPlanInit() for EMC_TASK_PLAN_INIT. The existing explicit calls next
to emcTaskAbort() are left as they are; a second call is a no-op.

tests/remap/mdi-remap-abort interrupts a waiting NGC and a waiting Python
remap with each of E-stop, machine off, interpreter re-init and a mode
change, and checks that milltask survives and MDI works afterwards.
